# Mario Kart 8 Deluxe Clean Asset Database

This folder is the public, clean database package for project development.

## Contents

- `database.json` is the source of truth for code.
- `images/` contains deterministic PNG assets.
- `README.md` explains the package schema and common usage.

For the current language-dependent frame inventory and recording checklist, see
[`docs/LANGUAGE_FRAME_COVERAGE.md`](docs/LANGUAGE_FRAME_COVERAGE.md). The proposed
Time Trials recording workflow is in
[`docs/TIMETRIAL_LANGUAGE_CAPTURE_PLAN.md`](docs/TIMETRIAL_LANGUAGE_CAPTURE_PLAN.md).
As of 2026-10-27 all 12 game languages have every Time Trials frame (16 templates,
including the new solo finish banner 085) and the three Switch Album/video bars;
230 of 240 language/frame slots are integrated. Only the LAN results banner (064)
is missing for the ten languages without a LAN recording (needs two Switches).
Languages with identical artwork share one cut-out through link groups. The
analyzer still needs locale-aware track OCR and name matching before
new-language analysis can be called complete.

The release zip contains only `database.json`, `README.md`, and `images/`. Local generated manifests may exist beside those files for rebuild/debug/audit work, but they are not part of the runtime zip.

## Image Policy

Final runtime assets are PNG files only. PNG was chosen because it works reliably with Python/Pillow, preserves alpha channels, and is easy to inspect in development tools. Source files may be WebP or PNG, but consumers must use this folder's PNG exports. `database.json` never references WebP files, and every public asset `path` points to a PNG.

## Build Pipeline

The final package is generated by `tools/build_final_database.py`:

1. Load the cleaned local database from `assets/clean_database/database.json`.
2. Load the legacy submission snapshot from `assets/database.json` for world-record and Nintendo Staff time-trial data.
3. Export trusted source images from the cleaned database and mariokart.info-derived assets into deterministic PNG paths under `assets/final/images/`.
4. Normalize public IDs, slugs, names, stats, track/cup relationships, and asset metadata.
5. Build vehicle body/glider variant relationships instead of flattening every color variant into the base part record.
6. Split item icons from the local item sheet using the exact top-left separator color and preserve the prepared crop boxes.
7. Parse saved mkwrs snapshots for 150cc and 200cc world records, including lap splits, coin counts, mushroom counts, and Baby Park's seven-lap format.
8. Add Nintendo Staff time-trial records from the legacy snapshot as official baseline records.
9. Write `database.json`, manifests, generated README, review HTML, and validation report.

The public database is the generated output. Source-only fields and legacy identifiers are kept in manifests for auditability, not in public records.

## Database Shape

Current schema version: `9`.

Schema version 9 keeps everything from version 8 and unifies language variants of HUD
templates into single records: a language-dependent template now has ONE id and index,
with its variants inside `assets.template.byLanguage` (mirroring localized `names`).
Every tracked game language is listed there; `null` is an open placeholder. A
contributor can select that slot in the guided cutout tool, save a Spanish cutout,
review it, and run the build/apply scripts to publish
`images/hud/templates/es/NNN_slug.png` and fill the database slot, without new ids,
renumbering, or schema changes. Each variant carries its own `anchor` and `source` (text widths differ
per language). The separate `*_nl` template records and the
`hudTemplateLanguageVariants` index are gone; detection refs point to the single ids.

Schema version 8 keeps everything from version 7 and adds the LAN result-screen deep
data, merged from the production-validated MK8DX Video Result Extractor project:
`detection.lanResultLayouts` (both 12-row scoreboard grids, digit-cell geometry, track-name
OCR ROI, frame-normalization contract), `detection.sevenSegment` (canonical 7-segment digit
spec shared by LAN digit grids and time-trial clock reading), `detection.characterFamilies`
(colour families + close-cutout groups needing variant refinement), and
`externalIds.videoResultExtractor` on cups/tracks/characters/variants so the extractor's
compact `game_catalog.json` can be regenerated from this database with
`tools/derive_game_catalog.py` (output in `catalog/`). At that stage hudTemplates grew to 75, then language variants were unified into 70 records in schema 9; subsequent additions brought the current total to 91. The stage added clean
composited score rank columns in white/black state, English + Dutch variants of the
results-in banners, Solo/Ghost option rows and Switch system-UI bars (negative gates,
group `system` / `detection.systemUi`), the ghost-data footer with its 30px sidebar
anchor shift, and the Dutch START!
go-signal (countdown digits are language-independent, the go word is not). Screen
elements that only need to be DETECTED (the score-frame, and one selected-option-row
template matched at both row anchors) are language/outcome-independent intersection
masks — only pixels identical across all observed variants, varying text excluded. Records may carry `kind` and `language`
fields).

Schema version 7 keeps everything from version 6 and adds `collections.hudTemplates` plus
`detection.timeTrialFlow`: 65 RGBA screen templates (menu chrome, countdown, in-race HUD
counters, timers, result-screen sidebar, character/vehicle-select, pause and LAN-tournament
markers) cut frame-exactly from own gameplay recordings, and a state-machine recipe that
turns template sightings into an accurate time-trial timeline (track + cc lock, ghost/solo
mode, race start/finish gates, coin/lap/mushroom rules, clock semantics). LAN-screen
markers live in `detection.lanMarkers`. See the "HUD Templates And Time-Trial Flow"
section below.

Schema version 6 keeps everything from version 5 and adds a top-level `detection` block: a compact,
machine-readable recipe for locating and identifying the loadout icons (character/body/wheel/glider)
in a 1280x720 Mario Kart 8 Deluxe Time-Trial HUD frame. It stores box sizes, the anchor offsets from
the player/ghost timetrail box per lap mode, reference-asset rules, and matcher parameters -- no image
data is stored, the reference templates are derived from `images/`. See the "Detection" section below.

Schema version 5 keeps everything from version 4 and makes character color variants consistent with vehicle body and glider variants. Character color recolors (Yoshi, Birdo, Shy Guy, and Inkling colors) are no longer flattened into `collections.characters`. Instead, `collections.characters` holds only distinct playable characters, and each recolor lives in the new `relationships.characterVariants` table linked to its base character. Time-trial loadouts gained an optional `characterVariantId`, and body/glider variants gained an optional `relatedCharacterVariantIds` to reference those color variants directly.

Schema version 4 was the previous baseline. It keeps typed time-trial IDs such as `world_record.time.150cc.mario_kart_stadium`, removes duplicate default vehicle PNGs, normalizes `source` fields to objects, exposes default item icons directly, marks MK8 in-game countries with `isMk8Country`, and documents the additive stat model.

The public database uses clean IDs, zero-based `index` values, localized `names`, optional `stats`, and asset references. Legacy identifiers and legacy submission IDs are intentionally not present in public records. Use `manifests/source_mapping.json` if source traceability is needed.

## Record Identity And Future User Submissions

Record IDs use typed namespaces and should not contain usernames or display names. User-facing names can change, collide, contain Unicode, or be reused, so they belong in fields such as `playerName` or a linked player record.

Recommended namespace examples:

```text
world_record.time.150cc.mario_kart_stadium
nintendo_staff.time.150cc.mario_kart_stadium
user_submission.time.<stable-submission-id>
```

Queries should use explicit fields such as `recordType`, `mode`, `trackId`, `playerId`, and `source.kind` instead of parsing meaning from the ID string. Current generated time-trial IDs follow this namespace pattern.

Future user-submitted records should be processed as separate submission records, not mixed into Nintendo Staff or world-record collections:

```json
{
  "id": "user_submission.time.01JZ8M7V6YQ5Z4",
  "recordType": "time_trial_submission",
  "mode": 150,
  "trackId": "track.mario_kart_stadium",
  "playerId": "player.01JZ8M7V6YQ5Z4",
  "timeMs": 92123,
  "dateMs": 1783468800000,
  "status": "pending_review"
}
```

Player identity should be stored separately from submissions:

```json
{
  "id": "player.01JZ8M7V6YQ5Z4",
  "displayName": "Nin★Chris"
}
```

This keeps official Staff records, mkwrs world records, and community submissions queryable without ID collisions.

## JSON Field Reference

Most records follow this shape:

```json
{
  "id": "character.mario",
  "index": 0,
  "slug": "mario",
  "names": {
    "en-US": "Mario",
    "en-GB": "Mario",
    "nl-NL": "Mario",
    "ja-JP": "マリオ",
    "ko-KR": "마리오",
    "zh-CN": "马力欧",
    "zh-TW": "瑪利歐"
  },
  "assets": {
    "menu": {
      "assetId": "asset.character.menu.mario",
      "path": "images/characters/menu/000_mario.png",
      "width": 128,
      "height": 128,
      "hasAlpha": true
    }
  }
}
```

Core fields:

- `id`: stable public identifier. Use this in application code and relationships.
- `index`: zero-based ordering index within that collection. This is for stable sorting, not source traceability.
- `slug`: readable URL/file-friendly name.
- `names`: localized display names. See **Localization** below for the 13 supported locales and their coverage.
- `assets`: named image references. Paths are relative to this folder.
- `assetId`: stable ID for one generated image asset.
- `width`, `height`, `hasAlpha`: image metadata from the generated PNG.
- `stats`: optional vehicle or character stat block when available.
- `statModel`: top-level metadata describing how stats should be added and capped.
- `bodyType`: vehicle body category, such as `Kart`, `Bike`, or `ATV`.
- `driftType`: body drift behavior, such as inward or outward drift.
- `aliases`: alternate country names when available.
- `officialName`: formal country name when available.
- `isOfficialItemListEntry`: whether an item concept is part of the official item list.

## Localization

**Console languages.** `consoleLanguages.languages` lists the 12 entries of the Nintendo Switch system language list in the console's order (English, Français, Deutsch, Español, Italiano, Nederlands, Português, Русский, 日本語, 繁體中文, 简体中文, 한국어). MK8DX follows the console language, and for English, French and Spanish also the system region: Europe gives the EUen/EUfr/EUes texts, North and South America the USen/USfr/USes texts, so the game has 15 localisations (`gameLocales`; texts and checks in `docs/GAME_MESSAGES.md`). The HUD templates were captured on a console set to region Europe. Each entry gives the template bucket `code` (as in `assets.template.byLanguage`), the console's own `label`, the `textLocales` to match printed names against and a display `flag` (in-game MK8 flags; China and Taiwan from `images/countries/language_flags/`). The two Chinese entries are labelled with the console's own neutral script names 简体中文 and 繁體中文.


The top-level `locales` array lists 13 locale codes for the languages supported by Mario Kart 8 Deluxe, in priority order:

`en-US`, `en-GB`, `nl-NL`, `fr-FR`, `ru-RU`, `de-DE`, `ja-JP`, `es-ES`, `it-IT`, `pt-PT`, `ko-KR`, `zh-CN`, `zh-TW`

**Coverage.** The gameplay/loadout entities, cups and tracks carry all 13 locales plus the two American game variants `fr-CA` and `es-419` (names from the NTSC columns of the translation sheet; `gameLocales[].nameLocales` says which locale each of the 15 game localisations prints). `fr-CA` and `es-419` are name locales only and are not in the top-level `locales` array. Items and item icons carry only the original three (`en-US`, `en-GB`, `nl-NL`):

| Localized (13 + `fr-CA`, `es-419`) | Base locales only (`en-US`, `en-GB`, `nl-NL`) |
| --- | --- |
| `characters` (54) | `items` (24) |
| `relationships.characterVariants` (28) | `relationships.itemIconVariants` (37) |
| `tracks` (96) | |
| `cups` (24) | |
| `vehicleBodies` (41) | |
| `vehicleTires` (22) | |
| `vehicleGliders` (15) | |
| `countries` (249, the 13 locales) | |

**Names and printed names.** `names[locale]` is the intended full name. `displayNames[locale]`, present only where it differs, is exactly what the console prints, e.g. the abbreviation `Lichtbl. Shy Guy` (de-DE), `Пич из роз. золота` (ru-RU), or `Villager` for both Villagers in British and American English. Match screen text against `displayNames[locale] ?? names[locale]`. Every printed character, colour variant, body, tire, glider, cup and track name was read on a Switch in all 12 console languages (region Europe) and in the US sessions for en-US, fr-CA and es-419; where the console and the translation sheet disagree, the console wins (`sources.consoleNameCheck`). Items that print the same text on purpose (Inkling colours, Villager in several locales, Link and Champion Link) cannot be told apart by their name; the icon decides.

Consumers should read `names[locale]` with a fallback to `en-US` (or your preferred base locale) when a given locale key is absent, so the base-locale-only collections degrade gracefully.

**Country names** are sourced from **Unicode CLDR** (`cldr-localenames-full` territory display names), keyed by each record's `iso2` (ISO 3166-1 alpha-2). Each country also carries `iso3` and `isoNumeric` (three digits as a string, e.g. `528` for the Netherlands, `442` for Luxembourg; `sources.countryIsoNumeric`). All 249 countries resolve in all 13 locales. This is documented in `sources.localizationCountries`.

Polish is excluded because [Nintendo does not list it among Mario Kart 8 Deluxe's supported languages](https://www.nintendo.com/en-ca/store/products/mario-kart-8-deluxe-can-105276/), even when the game is played on Nintendo Switch 2. The remaining added locales use translations sourced from the references listed under the top-level `sources` object (`localizationComponents`, `localizationTracks`, `localizationCountries`, `localizationMatrices`, `localizationRawAsianCommonMsbt`, `localizationCharacterVariants`).

**Not yet localized — `items`, `itemIconVariants`:** these carry only the three base locales. No clean, verified multilingual source for them exists in the cited references. Cup names were added for all locales from the translation sheet (Common.msbt) in October 2026 and each was read on the console.

Relationship records connect records without duplicating the parent object:

```json
{
  "id": "glider_variant.super_glider.000_black",
  "gliderId": "glider.super_glider",
  "variantSlug": "black",
  "relatedCharacterVariantIds": [
    "character_variant.yoshi.018_black",
    "character_variant.birdo.009_black",
    "character_variant.shy_guy.029_black"
  ],
  "asset": {
    "path": "images/vehicles/gliders/variants/super_glider/000_black.png"
  }
}
```

Color recolors of a character (black Yoshi, black Shy Guy, ...) are `characterVariants`, so a black
glider/kart references them through `relatedCharacterVariantIds` (as above), not `relatedCharacterIds`.
`relatedCharacterIds` is only for distinct base characters.

Relationship fields:

- `bodyId`, `gliderId`, `itemId`, `characterId`: parent record being extended or represented.
- `relatedCharacterIds`: base characters associated with that variant image.
- `relatedCharacterVariantIds`: character color variants (from `relationships.characterVariants`) associated with that variant image. A black kart color, for example, relates to the black color variants of Yoshi, Birdo, and Shy Guy.
- `appliesToAllCharacters`: variant applies generally instead of to specific character IDs.
- `variantSlug`: readable variant label, usually a color or source-specific variant name.
- `variantRole`: reason an item icon variant exists, such as `stacked_quantity` or `course_currency_replacement`.
- `quantity`: item count represented by an icon when relevant.
- `status`: review or availability marker, for example `unused_source_extra`.
- `unusedReason`: explanation for a retained but inactive source image.
- `sourceSheet`, `sourceBox`: source sprite sheet and crop rectangle for split item icons.
- `translationNote`: note explaining non-official or maintained translations.

Track and cup fields:

- `cupId`: cup that contains the track.
- `cupSlot`: zero-based position inside the cup.
- `trackIds`: ordered list of tracks inside a cup.
- `mkwrsName`: mkwrs.com track name used for world-record matching.

Time-trial record fields:

```json
{
  "id": "world_record.time.150cc.mario_kart_stadium",
  "recordType": "time_trial_world_record",
  "mode": 150,
  "trackId": "track.mario_kart_stadium",
  "timeMs": 93966,
  "dateMs": 1733587200000,
  "playerName": "Player",
  "countryId": "country.nl",
  "loadout": {
    "characterId": "character.mario",
    "bodyId": "body.standard_kart",
    "tireId": "tire.normal",
    "gliderId": "glider.super_glider"
  }
}
```

- `mode`: engine class, currently `150` or `200`.
- `recordType`: semantic type for querying, such as `time_trial_world_record` or `time_trial_nintendo_staff`.
- `trackId`: clean track ID.
- `timeMs`: total time in milliseconds. Format for display in the consuming app.
- `dateMs`: Unix epoch milliseconds.
- `playerName`: display name from the source.
- `countryId`: clean country ID when available.
- `loadout`: clean IDs for character, body, tire, and glider. When the record was set with a color recolor, `loadout.characterVariantId` points to the `relationships.characterVariants` entry and `loadout.characterId` stays on the base character.
- `source`: source category metadata, not source-only legacy IDs.
- `tieCount`: number of equal-time world-record rows represented by one public record. Tie source IDs are kept in `source_mapping.json`.
- `videoUrl`, `screenshotUrl`: optional proof/media links from the source.

## Stats

Stats are stored as additive in-game bar-value contributions. Consumers can calculate a loadout by adding character, body, tire, and glider values for each stat. MK8DX effectively caps useful stat values at `5.75` on the six-bar scale; totals above `5.75` should be treated as `5.75` for display and performance comparisons.

```json
{
  "stat": "miniTurbo",
  "rawTotal": 6.0,
  "effectiveValue": 5.75,
  "displayValue": 5.75
}
```

Mini-Turbo is included because it is a hidden but important stat. Lightweight setups can reach the same `5.75` effective cap for Mini-Turbo that heavy setups can reach for speed.

