## Items

The database includes `collections.items` for canonical item concepts and `relationships.itemIconVariants` for the actual icons split from:

```text
Input/Nintendo Switch - Mario Kart 8 Deluxe - Miscellaneous - Item Icons.png
```

The item sheet is split by using the exact top-left pixel color as the connected separator color. Crops are not trimmed tighter after detection; the prepared item boxes are preserved.

There are 37 generated item icon PNGs in `images/items/icons/`. Each canonical item exposes `defaultIconVariantId` and `assets.defaultIcon` when a default icon is available. Some icons are direct item icons, while others are variants that relate back to a canonical item:

- stacked quantity icons, such as double bananas or double shells
- course currency replacements, such as rupees and bells, which relate to Coin
- alternate or team-color icons, such as Piranha Plant color variants

Example canonical item default icon:

```json
{
  "id": "item.coin",
  "defaultIconVariantId": "item_icon.000_coin",
  "assets": {
    "defaultIcon": {
      "path": "images/items/icons/000_coin.png"
    }
  }
}
```

Example icon relationship:

```json
{
  "id": "item_icon.002_rupee",
  "itemId": "item.coin",
  "variantRole": "course_currency_replacement",
  "quantity": 1,
  "asset": {
    "path": "images/items/icons/002_rupee.png"
  }
}
```

## Character Color Variants

Several characters are pure color recolors that share the base character's stats and gameplay: they are one selectable character with a color swap, exactly like vehicle body and glider color variants. To stay consistent with `relationships.bodyVariants` and `relationships.gliderVariants`, these recolors are not stored as separate character records. `collections.characters` holds only distinct playable characters, and each recolor lives in `relationships.characterVariants`.

Recolor families are Yoshi, Birdo, Shy Guy, Inkling Girl, and Inkling Boy. The base character record already represents the default color (for example base Yoshi is green), so only the non-default colors appear as variants. Characters that look like recolors but have different stats, such as Metal Mario, Gold Mario, and Pink Gold Peach, are genuinely distinct characters and remain in `collections.characters`.

Each variant links back to its base character and carries its own menu and score images:

```json
{
  "id": "character_variant.yoshi.018_black",
  "characterId": "character.yoshi",
  "variantSlug": "black",
  "sourceIndex": 18,
  "names": {
    "en-US": "Black Yoshi",
    "en-GB": "Black Yoshi",
    "nl-NL": "Zwarte Yoshi"
  },
  "assets": {
    "menu": { "path": "images/characters/menu/018_black_yoshi.png" },
    "score": { "path": "images/characters/score/018_black_yoshi.png" }
  },
  "status": "active"
}
```

Character variant fields:

- `characterId`: base character this recolor belongs to.
- `variantSlug`: color label, such as `black` or `light_blue`.
- `sourceIndex`: original ordering index the recolor had before it was split out; also the numeric prefix of its image files.
- `names`, `assets`, `status`: same shape as a base character record.

To resolve a full character for display, use the base record from `collections.characters` for stats and the variant record for the color name and images. Body and glider color variants reference these entries through `relatedCharacterVariantIds`, and time-trial loadouts reference them through `loadout.characterVariantId`.

## Nintendo Staff Times

`collections.nintendoStaffTimes` preserves the official Nintendo Staff time-trial entries from the legacy submission snapshot. These are baseline Nintendo records, not community submissions. Each record links to the clean track, country, character, body, tire, and glider IDs where that source data is available.

Staff records use `recordType: "time_trial_nintendo_staff"` and `source.kind: "official_nintendo_staff"`. Staff dates are stored as Unix epoch milliseconds in `dateMs`. The old source `submissionId` values are kept only in `manifests/source_mapping.json`.

Since v5.3 every staff record carries `splits` in the World Record shape:
`lapCount`, `lapTimesMs` (three laps, seven on GCN Baby Park, summing exactly to
`timeMs`), `events` (`not_available`), and `coinCountsPerLap` /
`mushroomsPerLap` as `null` because the Select-a-Ghost panel does not show them.
`splits.source` says where the laps come from: the expanded Select-a-Ghost panel
of each staff ghost, captured over HDMI on a physical Switch (2026-10-25) and
read identically by the Web and Android scanners on that frame and on a phone
photo of the same panel. Where the panel contradicted a staff record (three
flags, two tires) the field shows what the panel shows and
`source.corrections` keeps the previous value with its evidence. Reproducible
with `python tools/add_staff_splits.py` (`--check` writes a candidate only).

Regular user submissions from the old database are intentionally not included in the clean public database.

## World Record Times And Splits

World-record total times are stored as `timeMs` only. Consumers should format that value for display, for example `93966` -> `1:33.966`. Record dates are stored as Unix epoch milliseconds in `dateMs`.

When mkwrs exposes split popups, records include `splits.lapTimesMs`, `splits.coinCountsPerLap`, and `splits.mushroomsPerLap`. The raw popup arguments are also retained in `splits.rawArguments`.

Per-coin and per-mushroom timing is modeled under `splits.events`. Current mkwrs data does not provide those exact timings, so generated records start with `events.status = "not_available"` and empty event arrays. When manually refined, use race-elapsed milliseconds in `timeMs`; optional `lap` can be added for grouping.

Coin events should use `delta` for additions or removals and `coinCountAfter` for the resulting coin count:

```json
{
  "timeMs": 12640,
  "lap": 1,
  "delta": 1,
  "coinCountAfter": 4,
  "source": "manual_review"
}
```

Mushroom events should store each use separately. Time trials can have 0 to 3 mushroom uses, so `mushroomIndex` is one-based within that run:

```json
{
  "timeMs": 48220,
  "lap": 2,
  "mushroomIndex": 1,
  "source": "manual_review"
}
```

Filled event example:

