## World Record Refresh Safety

World-record refresh is all-or-nothing. The builder fetches the 150cc and 200cc mkwrs pages into memory, validates both pages, and only then replaces the saved snapshots. If mkwrs is down, incomplete, corrupt, or structurally unexpected, the previous snapshots remain untouched.

Validation checks for 96 unique track names per mode, not exactly 96 physical table rows. This matters because mkwrs can show equal-time ties as separate table rows; for example, GBA Boo Lake can have two equal records by two different players. The database still stores one current WR per `trackId + mode`, with tied source submission IDs retained when they are available from the local source data.

## Detection

The top-level `detection` block (schema 6) is a small (~3 KB) config for reading a player's or ghost's
loadout out of a 1280x720 Time-Trial HUD frame. It does not store any pixels -- the reference templates
are built at load time from `images/` using the rules in the block.

- `boxSizes` -- ROI size per group: characters `48x48`, body/wheel/glider `80x50`, flags `55x38`.
- `anchor.offsets` -- `(dx,dy)` added to a player/ghost timetrail-box top-left to crop each icon, given
  per lap mode (`3lap` / `7lap`). Detect the mode by lap-index count: `<=6` = 3-lap, `>=7` = 7-lap.
  (Character and flag sit on the top row and do not shift with lap mode.)
- `referenceAssets` -- which asset each group uses (characters `score`; vehicles `default` + variants;
  flags `mk8Flag` for the 110 `isMk8Country` countries only), the `_default` exclusion, and
  `_default -> base` canonicalization.
- `variantResolution` -- body/glider color variant is fixed from the identified character via
  `relatedCharacterIds` / `relatedCharacterVariantIds` (a character determines the color of its parts).
- `scoreboard` -- grid for the RaceScore/TotalScore results screen (12-row post-race table): per-layout
  (`1p`/`2p`) character and flag crop coordinates. Characters are matched against the `characters`
  (score) collection with a small offset search (confidence separates real characters from Miis); the
  optional per-player flag is matched against the MK8 `flags` after a presence gate (contrast +
  horizontal-band edges) tells a flag from the empty row area.
- `params` -- alpha threshold, query offsets, confidence (`scoreMin`) and ambiguity (`marginMin`) cutoffs.

Technique in short: crop each icon at its anchored box, downscale each reference into that box, and
compare only at the reference's non-transparent (alpha) pixels so the HUD background is ignored. On a
120-identity benchmark this reads top-1 / top-3 100% at ~8 ms per loadout. The full matcher
implementation, usage, and rebuild steps live in the separate `MK8DX Icon Matcher` project.

Local flag-only identification is available in `tools/mk8_flag_matcher.py`. It loads the 110 official
MK8 country flags from `database.json`, returns the actual country metadata, checks exact PNG hashes
first, then uses a strict color-preserving template score with row/column/grid signatures. The matcher
fails closed as `ambiguous` when the top candidates are too close instead of guessing. This is required
for similar flags such as `129_lu` Luxembourg and `155_nl` Netherlands, where the decisive signal is
the darker blue stripe in the Netherlands asset.

```bash
python tools/mk8_flag_matcher.py identify images/countries/mk8_flags/129_lu.png
python tools/mk8_flag_matcher.py self-test
```

Note: characters are matched from `score` assets, so the three Mii characters (which have no `score`
icon) have no template and are not identifiable by this method -- a Mii character box yields a
low-confidence (unrecognized) result.

## HUD Templates And Time-Trial Flow

`collections.hudTemplates` holds 91 RGBA templates for identifying MK8DX
time-trial screens and HUD state in 1280x720 video, live (capture card) or from
recordings. Assets live under `images/hud/templates/`. Each record carries:

- `group` / `element` / `state`: `hud.coins` with states `00`-`10`, `countdown` with
  `3/2/1/go`, etc. States of one element are variants of the same on-screen thing.
- `anchor`: the exact `{x, y, w, h}` position in the source frame — templates are
  position-stable HUD elements, so matching can search a small window at the anchor
  instead of the whole frame.
- `kind`: `template` (matchable pixels) or `zone_reference` (marks WHERE variable
  content renders — e.g. the 12 roster rows on the LAN intro screen).
- `languageDependent` + `assets.template.byLanguage`: language-dependent templates
  keep one id/index; the per-language variants (each with its own `path`, `anchor`
  and `source`) live inside the record, with `null` placeholders for all supported
  game languages that have not been captured yet. Files live in per-language
  subfolders with identical filenames (`images/hud/templates/en/013_countdown_go.png`,
  `.../nl/013_countdown_go.png`, ...); language-independent templates stay in the
  folder root and keep the flat `assets.template` shape.
- `ocrZones` (banners only): transparent holes cut into the banner masks are the text
  zones, exported in frame coordinates with a `role` (`track_name`, `cup_name`,
  `race_counter`, `auxiliary`). The alpha mask itself encodes where to read.
- `source`: provenance (video, frame, `timeMs`, cutout id, whether the template was
  hand-refined). Staging data and full prose spec live in `cutouts/`
  (`collection.json`, `detection_logic.md`); the tagging tool is `tools/video_cutout/`.

`detection.timeTrialFlow` is the machine-readable state machine consuming those
templates: screen gates (character select via the roster, vehicle select, cup screen,
track confirm), the 150cc/200cc banner-badge consistency rule, OCR-based track naming
matched against `tracks[].names`, ghost-vs-solo mode from the highlighted option row,
race-start co-occurrence conditions, in-race rules (laps strictly increase, coins move
both ways with orange max at 10, mushroom use triggers and the ring-exit signal), clock
freeze semantics, lap-delta templates (ghost runs only), the result-screen sidebar as
the completed-run gate, and pause/abort handling. Times themselves are read as
7-segment pixel zones by the consumer (the separate MK8TimetrialsLiveFeed project),
anchored at the clock/delta templates.

The `lan` template group plus `detection.lanMarkers` cover the 12-player LAN
tournament screens (track intro with roster zones and track-name OCR holes, the
between-races race counter banner, and the score-screen frame/rank-column gates).
These are positive markers only — the LAN flow state machine is not specified yet.
`detection.lanResultLayouts` carries the full scoreboard geometry (12-row grids for
both capture layouts, digit cells, normalization contract) and `detection.sevenSegment`
the canonical digit-segment spec, both production-validated in the MK8DX Video Result
Extractor project. `tools/derive_game_catalog.py` regenerates that project's compact
`game_catalog.json` from this database (join table: `externalIds.videoResultExtractor`,
written by `tools/enrich_from_extractor.py`).

The `ghostreplay` group contains ten menu templates, captured for all 12 languages:
selected and unselected rows for Continue Replay, Replay from Start, Race against
Ghost, and Quit Replay, plus the selected Start Replay confirmation and View Ghost
button. Their source recordings are 1920x1080; the
published PNGs and anchors are normalized to the collection's 1280x720 frame.
The original source-frame size remains in each template's `source.frameSize`.

`detection.ghostMenuPanel` holds the fixed ROIs of the Select-a-Ghost panel on the ghost
menu (1280x720): the header (character, name, flag, total time), one `trackName` box
for the bottom banner (same for every layout), and for the expanded
panel the lap indexes, lap times, body/tire/glider icons and part names in a `3lap` and a
`7lap` layout, plus the `layoutDetect` probe that tells them apart from a collapsed
panel. 3-lap boxes were placed in the COCO geometry editor; 7-lap boxes were measured
against them (same header and x, lap pitch 32 instead of 40, part rows +98 px).

## Nameplates

A nameplate is the binary mask of a name exactly as the console prints it: the name bar under
the character, vehicle and cup screens, and the name line of the four track tiles on the cup
screen. Each character, colour variant, body, tire, glider, cup and track carries
`assets.nameplate.byLocale` with one entry per game locale (`path`, `width`, `height`, `text`
= the printed text without the track tag, `anchor` in the 1920x1080 frame, `source` with the
walk, recording and shot, `sharedWith` for ids that print the same text). Files live in
`images/nameplates/<locale>/<kind>/` with the item's usual file name. A locale without its own capture
links to the European entry (`source.linkedFrom`) when the printed text is identical, otherwise
the slot is `null`.

`detection.nameplates` is the matching contract: the regions, the mask steps, IoU matching with a
2 px search, the tag group as a hard filter for tracks (Mario Circuit and Rainbow Road print the
same name under several tags), and the decision rule with an OCR fallback. Built from the
2026-10-28 HDMI atlas walks (all 12 console languages on region Europe; English, French and
Spanish again on region The Americas for en-US, fr-CA and es-419) with
`tools/add_nameplates.py`; the item of each shot comes from its position on screen and OCR only
checked it. Evaluated on 4916 shots: 4916 correct, 0 wrong, 0 undecided (879 of them not used as
a reference). Every locale slot is filled; four fr-CA tires link to the identical fr-FR plate.
Not captured: Gold Mario and the Gold Kart (not unlocked on the capture console). Miis print the player's Mii name and have no nameplate. All captures come from a Nintendo Switch (first generation, system 23.0.1; `detection.nameplates.captureConsole`, `source.consoleModel`); a Nintendo Switch 2 is untested and may render menus differently.

## Known Special Cases

- Mii Light, Mii Medium, and Mii Heavy are customizable and may not have fixed score icons.
- Some body/glider variant images exist in source graphics but are not assigned to active character relationships. They are kept with `status: "unused_source_extra"`.
- Default body/glider variants that only repeated the base asset and carried no character mapping are omitted to avoid duplicate PNG files.
- `mk8Flag` means an actual in-game MK8DX flag asset. Generic ISO flags are not used as fallback.
- Tracks keep `mkwrsName` so world-record rows can be matched to mkwrs.com naming.

